   "source": [
    "# Module 13: Measure Theory & Advanced Probability\n",
    "\n",
    "Standard probability theory (using calculus/integration) breaks down when dealing with continuous variables, infinite-dimensional spaces (like stochastic processes), or sets of probability zero. Measure theory provides the rigorous foundation of probability needed for advanced ML concepts like continuous normalizing flows, diffusion models, and convergence guarantees.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Contents\n",
    "1. $\\sigma$-Algebras, Measurable Spaces, and Measure Spaces\n",
    "2. Probability Measures & Borel Sets\n",
    "3. Lebesgue Integration & Convergence Theorems (Monotone, Dominated)\n",
    "4. Radon-Nikodym Theorem & Likelihood Ratios\n",
    "5. Martingales & Convergence\n",
    "6. Stochastic Calculus & Itô's Lemma"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. $\\sigma$-Algebras, Measurable Spaces, and Measure Spaces\n",
    "\n",
    "To define probability rigorously, we cannot assign a probability to *every* subset of our sample space $\\Omega$ (due to paradoxes like Vitali sets). We must restrict ourselves to a family of \"allowable\" subsets called a $\\sigma$-algebra.\n",
    "\n",
    "### $\\sigma$-Algebra\n",
    "A collection $\\mathcal{F}$ of subsets of $\\Omega$ is a **$\\sigma$-algebra** if:\n",
    "1. $\\emptyset \\in \\mathcal{F}$\n",
    "2. If $A \\in \\mathcal{F}$, then its complement $A^c \\in \\mathcal{F}$ (closed under complement)\n",
    "3. If $A_1, A_2, \\dots \\in \\mathcal{F}$, then $\\bigcup_{i=1}^\\infty A_i \\in \\mathcal{F}$ (closed under countable union)\n",
    "\n",
    "The pair $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F})$ is a **measurable space**.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Measure\n",
    "A function $\\mu: \\mathcal{F} \\to [0, \\infty]$ is a **measure** if:\n",
    "- $\\mu(\\emptyset) = 0$\n",
    "- For any countable sequence of disjoint sets $A_1, A_2, \\dots \\in \\mathcal{F}$:\n",
    "  $$\\mu\\left( \\bigcup_{i=1}^\\infty A_i \\right) = \\sum_{i=1}^\\infty \\mu(A_i) \\quad \\text{(Countable Additivity)}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The triple $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mu)$ is a **measure space**."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Probability Measures & Borel Sets\n",
    "\n",
    "A **probability measure** $P$ is a measure on $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F})$ such that $P(\\Omega) = 1$. The triple $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, P)$ is a **probability space**.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Borel $\\sigma$-Algebra\n",
    "For $\\mathbb{R}^d$, the **Borel $\\sigma$-algebra** $\\mathcal{B}$ is the smallest $\\sigma$-algebra containing all open sets (or open intervals). Any subset of $\\mathbb{R}^d$ we encounter in real-world ML is a Borel set.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Lebesgue Measure\n",
    "The unique measure $\\lambda$ on $(\\mathbb{R}, \\mathcal{B})$ that matches our intuitive notion of length for intervals:\n",
    "$$\\lambda([a, b]) = b - a$$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Lebesgue Integration & Convergence Theorems\n",
    "\n",
    "Riemann integration partition the $x$-axis (domain), which fails for highly discontinuous functions (e.g. the Dirichlet function). Lebesgue integration partitions the $y$-axis (range) instead, defining the integral in terms of measures of sets:\n",
    "$$\\int f d\\mu = \\lim \\sum y_i \\mu(f^{-1}(y_i))$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Key Convergence Theorems in Probability:\n",
    "1. **Monotone Convergence Theorem (MCT)**: If $f_n \\ge 0$ and $f_n \\uparrow f$ pointwise, then:\n",
    "   $$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\int f_n d\\mu = \\int f d\\mu$$\n",
    "2. **Dominated Convergence Theorem (DCT)**: If $f_n \\to f$ pointwise and $|f_n| \\le g$ for all $n$ where $g$ is integrable ($\\int g d\\mu < \\infty$), then:\n",
    "   $$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\int f_n d\\mu = \\int f d\\mu$$\n",
    "   This justifies exchanging limits and expectations, e.g., $\\lim_n \\mathbb{E}[X_n] = \\mathbb{E}[\\lim_n X_n]$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Radon-Nikodym Theorem & Likelihood Ratios\n",
    "\n",
    "Given two measures $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ on the same measurable space $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F})$, we say $\\nu$ is **absolutely continuous** w.r.t. $\\mu$ (written $\\nu \\ll \\mu$) if:\n",
    "$$\\mu(A) = 0 \\implies \\nu(A) = 0 \\quad \\forall A \\in \\mathcal{F}$$\n",
    "This means $\\nu$ cannot place mass where $\\mu$ sees nothing.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Radon-Nikodym Theorem\n",
    "If $\\nu \\ll \\mu$ and both are $\\sigma$-finite measures, there exists a unique measurable function $f \\ge 0$ (called the **Radon-Nikodym derivative**, written $\\frac{d\\nu}{d\\mu}$) such that:\n",
    "$$\\nu(A) = \\int_A f d\\mu \\quad \\forall A \\in \\mathcal{F}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### AI Connections:\n",
    "- **Probability Densities**: A continuous PDF $p(x)$ is simply the Radon-Nikodym derivative of the probability measure w.r.t. the Lebesgue measure: $p(x) = \\frac{dP}{d\\lambda}$.\n",
    "- **Likelihood Ratios**: The likelihood ratio used in importance sampling or GANs is the Radon-Nikodym derivative of the target distribution w.r.t. the proposal distribution: $\\frac{dP_{target}}{dP_{proposal}}$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Martingales & Convergence\n",
    "\n",
    "A sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2, \\dots$ is a **martingale** w.r.t. a filtration $\\mathcal{F}_n$ (representing information up to time $n$) if:\n",
    "1. $\\mathbb{E}[|X_n|] < \\infty$ for all $n$\n",
    "2. $X_n$ is $\\mathcal{F}_n$-measurable\n",
    "3. $\\mathbb{E}[X_{n+1} | \\mathcal{F}_n] = X_n$ (the best prediction of the future is the present)\n",
    "\n",
    "Martingales are used to model fair games, stock prices, and convergence analysis of SGD updates."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Stochastic Calculus & Itô's Lemma\n",
    "\n",
    "A **Wiener Process** (Brownian Motion) $W_t$ satisfies $W_t - W_s \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, t-s)$. It is continuous but nowhere differentiable, so standard calculus fails. We use stochastic integration instead.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Itô's Lemma\n",
    "If $x_t$ is an Itô drift-diffusion process: $dx_t = f(x_t, t)dt + g(x_t, t)dW_t$, and $V(x, t)$ is a twice-differentiable scalar function, then $dV_t$ is given by:\n",
    "$$dV_t = \\left( \\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial t} + f \\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial x} + \\frac{1}{2} g^2 \\frac{\\partial^2 V}{\\partial x^2} \\right) dt + g \\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial x} dW_t$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The extra term $\\frac{1}{2} g^2 \\frac{\\partial^2 V}{\\partial x^2}$ comes from the fact that $(dW_t)^2 \\to dt$ (quadratic variation of Brownian motion is non-zero). This is the stochastic chain rule, crucial for diffusion models."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
